window.docContents[113].resolve({"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions--Smooth-multiplication":{"contents":"Every smooth scalar coefficient  defines a continuous linear operator on\nthe LF test-function space,Multiplication does not enlarge the test function's topological support.\n\nTwo facts about  are needed downstream. Its Leibniz rule is what becomes\nthe product rule for  after transposition, and its support inclusion is\nwhat allows smooth multiplication to preserve vanishing regions of\ndistributions.\n\nFor every direction ,\n\nMathlib's ContDiffMapSupportedIn.bilinLeftCLM supplies continuous\nmultiplication on each fixed compact-support stage. The LF universal property\nTestFunction.limitCLM assembles those operators into . The support\ninclusion follows from the support of a pointwise product, while Mathlib's\nFréchet product rule gives the displayed directional-derivative identity.\n\nA distribution is multiplied by a smooth coefficient by moving the coefficient\nonto the test function. Continuity of  on the LF space makes the\ntransposed operator continuous.\n\nThe compact-convergence transpose of  defines multiplication of\ndistributions by smooth coefficients:The construction applies to distributions valued in any real topological\nmodule satisfying Mathlib's standard continuity assumptions.\n\nThe product rule for  follows from the test-function Leibniz rule and the\nsign convention of the distributional derivative; the term  appears\nbecause  acting on  also differentiates the coefficient.\n\nThe multiplier has the expected pairing and distributional Leibniz laws:\n\nThe action formula is the defining formula of Mathlib's compact-convergence\ntranspose. Apply the distributional derivative to a test function and use the\ntest-function Leibniz rule. The negative-transpose convention moves the\nderivative from the distribution to the test function and yields the two\nterms in the displayed formula.\n\nMultiplying by  can only shrink support. An operation on test functions\nthat does not enlarge supports transposes to an operation on distributions that\npreserves every vanishing region, and the same mechanism applies to\nconstant-coefficient operators.\n\nSmooth multiplication preserves every vanishing region and every compact\ncarrier. In particular,\n\nIf a test function is supported in a vanishing region for , multiplication\nby  keeps its support in the same region. The transpose action formula\ntherefore makes  vanish there as well. The intrinsic-support inclusion\nfollows from Mathlib's vanishing characterization, and the same compact carrier\ncan be reused unchanged.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Canonical distributions","header":"3.4.1.7. Smooth multiplication","id":"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions--Smooth-multiplication"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Stokes-and-Incompressible-Navier___Stokes--A-certified-forced-shear-flow":{"contents":"Let\n\n\n\nThe velocity is divergence-free, and  because the field\ndepends only on  while advection points in the  direction. Since\n, the forcing balances viscosity.\n\nThe sine mode in the parabolic chapter decayed because this same Laplacian\nwas left unbalanced. Here forcing keeps the profile stationary. The symbolic\ncalculation can therefore reuse familiar scalar derivatives; the fluid\ncontent is the additional check that incompressibility and convection behave\nas claimed.\n\nThe verified CAS proves the exact identitieson all of . The momentum theorem is also stored as a\nzero-error residual certificate. CEGA projects that checked object into the\nordinary residual goal. Here cega selects the certificate's proved sound\nfield and checks that its residual, domain, and error budget match the target;\nno derivative is recomputed and no external solver is called.\n\nThe divergence is , and\n makes convection vanish. Finally,\n. The reflected expressions therefore normalize to\nzero componentwise, which is exactly the proposition proved by cas.\n\nThe certificate closes an ordinary proof goal:\n\n-- Project a checked zero residual into the proof goal.\nopen CAS.Certificate\nopen NavierStokes.Symbolic\n\nexample :\n    ResidualBound\n      shearNavierStokesMomentumResidual2.vectorFn\n      Set.univ 0 := by\n  cega using shearNavierStokesResidualCert2\n\n\nFor every integration domain and every velocity component,The integrand is the combined analytic residual, which vanishes identically,\nso the identity needs no integrability of the separate terms.\n\nThe semantic bridge identifies the analytic integrand with the evaluated\nsymbolic residual. That residual vanishes at every point, so its integral over\nthe chosen domain is zero.\n\nFor an approximate or numerically generated field, a validated backend may\ninstead supply a positive error,\n\n\n\nand CEGA projects such a certificate in the same way, proving a uniform\nresidual estimate.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Stokes and Navier–Stokes Flow\u0009Stokes and Incompressible Navier–Stokes","header":"8.5.1.1. A certified forced shear flow","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Stokes-and-Incompressible-Navier___Stokes--A-certified-forced-shear-flow"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Bounded-Domains-and-Improved-Regularity/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Bounded-Domains-and-Improved-Regularity--Time-Dependent-Divergence-Form":{"contents":"Variable-coefficient heat equations are most naturally expressed through\ntheir spatial bilinear forms. At almost every time, the form pairs a trial\nstate in  with a test state in the same space and returns the\ndual action of the elliptic operator. Time dependence is then handled through\nstrong measurability and uniform operator bounds rather than pointwise\nclassical derivatives of the coefficients.\n\nLower-order terms generally destroy strict coercivity. The correct estimate\nis a Gårding inequality: coercivity is recovered after adding a multiple of\nthe  norm. With the shift kept in the statements, the abstract\nnonautonomous evolution theory gives the weak equation, a continuous \nrepresentative, an energy inequality, and uniqueness.\n\nThe nonautonomous form is an operator-valued extension ofthrough the principal, first-order, and zeroth-order termsThe coefficient data are bounded operators on the spatial  space.\nPointwise  coefficients give multiplication operators, and nonlocal\nbounded coefficient operators are allowed as well.\n\nBecause the form is an operator-valued function of time with values in\n, it can be inserted directly\ninto the abstract nonautonomous evolution identity. The weak solution for\nthe full equation is therefore the same integrated Gelfand-triple notion\nused for the Dirichlet heat equation, with  in place of the Dirichlet\nform.\n\nThe time-dependent divergence-form weak solution is the same integrated\nGelfand-triple notion, specialized to the form . For , the\nstate  is a Bochner  class, the tested form and\nforcing terms are integrable, and the initial datum enters through the\nboundary term in time integration by parts.Under the form-measurability and boundedness hypotheses of\n, this class has a continuous\n representative . For\n, it satisfiesThe integrals use the energy-space Bochner representative ; the endpoint\nvalue  belongs to the continuous pivot representative. The\nDirichlet and divergence-form problems share this one weak-solution\npredicate.\n\nLower-order terms can prevent the energy identity from closing as it did for\nthe Dirichlet form: the general estimate leaves a term\n that need not be absorbable by the\nunshifted coercive part. The endpoint estimate is therefore a\nGårding-type inequality with two constants, the coercivity fraction\n and the mass shift , instead of the single constant of\nthe Dirichlet case.\n\nThe shift has a concrete meaning in time. In pivot-space notation, set\n in . The product rule gives\n\n\n\nThe added mass term is the coercive shift of the spatial form. Solving this\nequation and multiplying back by  recovers the original\nproblem and its initial datum. For , undoing the shift costs at\nmost  on the finite interval; it need not preserve decay.\nThe measurable-form theorem\ncarries out this change of variables in the integrated weak formulation.\n\nThe endpoint estimate consists of the weak  time derivative, continuous\n representative, initial trace, residual equation, and Gårding energy\ninequalityThe last term is left for Grönwall, since the unshifted form need not be\ncoercive.\n\nUniform ellipticity of the principal part and uniform bounds on the\nlower-order coefficients determine both constants explicitly: the theorem\nreturns  and a shift computed from the ellipticity\nconstant and the lower-order bounds.\n\nOn a finite interval  and a bounded open set , assume uniform\nellipticity of the principal part and uniform bounds on the lower-order\nterms throughout . Assume the induced form is a.e. strongly measurable\nas a map into  with its operator\nnorm, and uniformly bounded in that norm on . Then every\n forcing and  initial datum have a\nunique weak solution.The result includes a continuous  representative, weak time derivative,\nthe variational residual equation, and the Gårding energy inequality with\nits lower-order mass term.\n\nShift the Gårding form by its lower-order mass constant, apply the abstract\nnonautonomous Galerkin theorem, undo the exponential shift, and use\nLions–Magenes for the continuous pivot representative and endpoint control.\n\nWhen the coefficients do not depend on time, the same equation also\ngenerates a semigroup. The Gårding shift  that restores coercivity\nreappears as an exponential growth rate: the semigroup of the full operator\nis  times the contraction semigroup of the shifted one.\n\nLet  be open, let ,\nand let  be uniformly elliptic with constant . Write\n with its Dirichlet form\n. With the explicit Gårding shift  computed from ,\n and , the form satisfies\n, and there is a\nstrongly continuous semigroup  on  withIts generator is  with the weak Dirichlet domain:  has generator\nvalue  at  exactly when  is the class of some \nwith  for all .\n\nThe Gårding inequality makes  coercive on\n, so the form construction behind\n gives a contraction\nsemigroup  generated by . Then\n has generator , with the same domain.\nThe first-order term makes  nonsymmetric, which the Hille–Yosida\nargument does not need: coercivity alone bounds the resolvents.\n\nLet  be bounded and open with a  graph boundary, .\nLet  be  up to the boundary with\n for , and let\n,  be continuous up to the boundary. Then the semigroup of\n for these coefficients satisfies\n, its generator domain is exactly\n, and on that domain the generator acts as\n computed with strong second derivatives.\n\nOne inclusion is elliptic boundary regularity: if  solves\n weakly with , then . For the other, an\n function can be integrated by parts against every\n using the weak Leibniz rule for , which identifies\nits form with the pairing against the strong expression . The\ncoefficient classes in  and  are computed from the\ngiven functions, so no extension of the coefficients is assumed.\n\nThe same Galerkin machinery also handles a nonlinear reaction term, as long\nas the reaction is globally Lipschitz. Evans treats this system in §9.2.1 of\n.\n\nLet  be bounded and open, , , and\nlet  be globally Lipschitz. For every\n the system  with zero\nboundary values has a unique weak solution\n: it has a weak time derivative\n, a representative continuous from  into\n with value  at , and for almost every Two such solutions with the same initial value have the same class, the same\nderivative, and equal continuous representatives on . For\n the same conclusions hold with initial value\n.\n\nThe abstract theorem,\nthe Lipschitz-reaction existence theorem,\napplies to any Gårding form on a Gelfand triple. Given a trajectory\n, solve the linear problem with forcing  by the Galerkin theorem.\nThis defines a map  whose fixed points are the solutions. Replace\nthe unknown by : the rescaled reaction has the same\nLipschitz constant, because the exponential factors cancel in its difference\nestimate, while the linear part gains the coercive term . For\n large the map is a contraction on the whole interval\n, and the Banach fixed point theorem gives existence and uniqueness\nin the integrated formulation. The Gelfand evolution theorem then supplies\nthe derivative in  and the continuous representative.The exponential weight makes the contraction global on , with no\nrestarting. The value  may be nonzero, since a constant offset is square\nintegrable on a finite interval, and no boundary regularity of  is\nused.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Bounded Domains and Improved Regularity","header":"6.4.2. Time-Dependent Divergence Form","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Bounded-Domains-and-Improved-Regularity/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Bounded-Domains-and-Improved-Regularity--Time-Dependent-Divergence-Form"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--Null-Lagrangians-and-Constraints--Constrained-minimization":{"contents":"A constraint  given by a differentiable functional is the simplest\ncase, and the finite-dimensional rule survives in a Banach space: at a\nconstrained extremum the derivative of the energy is a multiple of the\nderivative of the constraint, provided that the constraint derivative is\nnonzero and both functionals have the strict differentiability required below.\n\nLet  be a real Banach space and  strictly\ndifferentiable at , with . If  is a local extremum of\n on the level set , then  for\nsome .\n\nThe implicit function argument gives coefficients  with\n. If  were zero then  and , which\nis excluded. Take .\n\nThe incompressibility constraint  is pointwise, not a single\nlevel set, and no multiplier rule is needed for existence. Weak continuity of\nthe determinant does the work: if  almost everywhere and\n in , , then \nin , and a weak limit of the constant function  is . The\nadmissible class is weakly closed although it is not convex.\n\nLet , , be bounded and open with \ngraph boundary, let , and let  be , convex in ,\nwith  for some . If\n has  almost everywhere, the\nextended energy  attains its minimum among maps\n with  almost everywhere. When \ndoes not depend on the value, the same holds in the class of maps with trace\n and , for every boundary datum  for which that\nclass is nonempty; if one admissible map has finite energy, the minimizer's\ndensity is integrable and its energy is finite.\n\nRun the direct method in the affine class of the given admissible map. The\nconstraint set is weakly sequentially closed by the weak continuity of the\ndeterminant, and the energy is weakly lower semicontinuous because it is\nconvex in . A bounded minimizing sequence in the constraint set has a\nweak limit that stays in it.\n\nThe sphere constraint  is the other classical example. Strong\n compactness preserves unit length and gives a minimizer when the\nadmissible class is nonempty. Deriving its equation needs a different\nargument. A variation  leaves the sphere, so it\nis projected back: the variation is , where  is a smooth map\nthat agrees with  on the annulus .\nFor a bounded direction  and small ,  stays in that annulus\nand the projected map again has unit length and the same boundary values.\n\nLet  be bounded and open with  graph\nboundary. Choose a smooth compactly supported map\n agreeing with  on\n. Let  have\n almost everywhere and let\n be bounded by . For  the\nprojected variation  has unit length almost everywhere, and if\n it has the boundary values of . The Dirichlet energy\n of the projected variation is differentiable at\n with derivative\n\nThe graph-domain hypotheses supply the Sobolev extension operator and inward\npartition used for composition and preservation of the boundary values.\nThe smooth compactly supported cutoff has bounded derivative and Lipschitz\n.\nSince , the chain rule gives\n, and at  the derivative of the\nnormalization  at a unit vector is the projection\n. Differentiating  gives , which turns\nthe projected term into . Differentiation under the integral\nsign is justified by an integrable bound built from the Lipschitz bound on\n.\n\nLet  be bounded and open with  graph\nboundary, and let . Assume the classis nonempty. Then the Dirichlet energy attains its minimum on\n. Every such minimizer  satisfiesThis is the weak form of the harmonic map equation\n. Nonemptiness of  is a hypothesis.\n\nFor existence, an energy-bounded minimizing sequence has bounded gradients.\nPoincaré's inequality controls the zero-boundary differences from ,\nso weak compactness gives an  limit with the same boundary values.\nRellich compactness gives strong  convergence, preserving unit length\nalmost everywhere, and weak lower semicontinuity gives minimality.\nThis existence argument itself needs only a bounded open domain of positive\ndimension.For the equation, the projected variations are admissible competitors, so the energy along\nthem has a minimum at  and its derivative there vanishes. The previous\ntheorem computes that derivative. An essentially bounded test field has an\nalmost-everywhere bound , which is all the variation theorem asks for,\nand the graph boundary supplies the Sobolev extension that the composition\nwith  needs.\n\nThe multiplier in the harmonic map equation is , a function rather\nthan a number. Formally it is the multiplier for a constraint imposed at\nevery point; here it comes out of the projected variation directly.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009Null Lagrangians and Constraints","header":"9.3.3.3. Constrained minimization","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--Null-Lagrangians-and-Constraints--Constrained-minimization"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--The-Gagliardo___Slobodeckij-condition":{"contents":"Fractional regularity can also be measured by comparing values at pairs of\npoints. For , the intrinsic squared seminorm is\n\n\n\nNearby pairs receive the greatest weight. For a smooth compactly supported\nfunction, the numerator is bounded by a constant times  near\nthe diagonal. Radial integration of the separation variable then gives\n, finite precisely when .\nFor distant pairs on the whole space, the  bound on translations\nleaves the tail , finite when .\nThese two calculations explain the interval  in which first\ndifferences measure fractional Sobolev regularity.\n\nFor a raw real function , defineIts square has exactly the classical Gagliardo kernelThe numerator vanishes on the diagonal, so the implemented quotient is zero\nthere for every ; no separate diagonal convention is needed.  The map\n is linear even at points where the denominator vanishes.\n\nSquare integrability of  over  is the Gagliardo\ncondition. Its formal counterpart has three layers: a predicate on raw\nfunctions, a partially defined operator on  classes, and a completed\ngraph space.\n\nThe Gagliardo condition on  isFor , this is the usual Sobolev–Slobodeckij membership condition.\nThe corresponding raw functions form gagliardoSubmodule.  On almost-everywhere\nclasses, the difference quotient is the partially defined linear operatorThe completed intrinsic space GagliardoSpace is the graph of  inside\nthe Hilbert productThe extended-real integral gagliardoSq records the square of the seminorm,\nwhile raw membership is phrased with MemLp so that measurability and closure\nunder linear operations come from Mathlib's  API.\n\nTwo formulations of the same finiteness condition coexist: membership of\n in , and finiteness of the extended-real integral\ngagliardoSq. Their agreement, established next, is what allows the\nwhole-space Fourier identity below to be transferred to the membership\npredicate.\n\nFor every real  and every set , gagliardoSq is exactly the\nsquare of the raw extended  seminorm eLpNorm' of . Consequently,\nMemGagliardo is equivalent to square integrability of  on \ntogether with finiteness of gagliardoSq.No separate measurability assumption is needed: the  hypothesis on\n makes both coordinate pullbacks almost everywhere strongly measurable,\nand division by the measurable distance power preserves that property.\nUnder this hypothesis, eLpNorm' agrees with eLpNorm for the difference quotient.\n\nMathlib's formula expressing an  seminorm squared as a lintegral gives\nthe first identity. The forward implication extracts finiteness from\nMemLp; the reverse implication combines the finite lintegral with the\nmeasurability inherited from the base  function.\n\nCompleteness of the graph space is equivalent to closedness of , and\nclosedness is a pointwise matter: along a subsequence converging almost\neverywhere, the  limit of the difference quotients of  is the\ndifference quotient of the limit of the .\n\nThe operator  is closed for every real  and every set .\nConsequently GagliardoSpace is a complete real Hilbert space.  Its two\ncontinuous coordinate maps satisfyandThe first coordinate is injective and its range is exactly\n.  A raw function satisfies MemGagliardo exactly\nwhen it agrees almost everywhere on  with the first coordinate of\nan element of GagliardoSpace.\n\nSuppose graph elements  converge in the product  space.\nFrom  in , choose a subsequence converging pointwise\nalmost everywhere.  From convergence of the second coordinates, choose a\nfurther subsequence converging pointwise almost everywhere on\n.  Pulling the first convergence through both product\nprojections shows that the pointwise limit of the difference quotients is\n.  Uniqueness of limits identifies it with the second \ncoordinate, so the graph is closed.  A closed subspace of the Hilbert\n product is complete, and the norm and inner-product formulas are\nthe product formulas.  The graph relation gives injectivity and the\nraw-function characterization.\n\nThe same three layers are defined for every exponent \nand every real normed target . The quotient becomes\n, both  spaces become \nspaces, and the graph of  is closed for the same reason, so the graph\nspace is a Banach space whenever  is complete. The Hilbert case above is\n, . The trace theorems at the end of the chapter use the\nexponent  and order .\n\nBoth coordinates of a graph element are  functions on a restricted\nmeasure, so shrinking  restricts both, and restriction of the base\ncoordinate is compatible with forming the difference quotient. This yields the\nsame functorial contraction structure that the restriction space carries.\n\nIf , restriction of representatives defines a\ncontinuous linear contractionIts first coordinate is the canonical restriction\n, and its second coordinate is the canonical\nrestriction\n.  Packaging a\nraw function by ofFunction commutes with this map.  Restriction to the same\ndomain is the identity, and restrictions compose along chains of domain\ninclusions.\n\nRestricted Lebesgue measure and its product measure are monotone under\n.  Mathlib's canonical restriction maps on\n are therefore contractions.  Almost-everywhere equality of the first\ncoordinates implies almost-everywhere equality of their difference\nquotients, so the restricted pair remains in the graph of .  Applying\nthe two coordinate contraction estimates to the graph norm proves the norm\nbound.  Identity, composition, and compatibility with ofFunction follow\nfrom equality of the first  coordinates.\n\nThe definition is stated for every real , but only for  does the\nkernel behave like a fractional derivative. A few elementary members and one\ndegenerate exponent make the point.\n\nOn a finite-measure domain, every constant belongs because its difference\nquotient vanishes. On a measurable domain , every function vanishing\non  also belongs, regardless of its values outside . At\n the denominator exponent is zero andfor finite-measure .  This concrete degeneracy is why the formal\ndefinition is named MemGagliardo, not unconditionally MemHs.\n\nFor constants, and for functions vanishing on a measurable , the\ndifference quotient is zero almost everywhere on .\nAt , it reduces to . On a finite-measure product domain,\nthe two pullbacks of an\n function are in , and their difference is in ;\nthe converse is the base  clause of the definition.\n\nThe seminorm detects every continuous function that is not constant on an\nopen set. Continuity is needed because the condition is stated for raw\nfunctions: the indicator of a single point is not constant, yet its seminorm\nis zero.\n\nFor every real , the seminorm is monotone in the set:\n when\n. If  is open and a continuous real\nfunction  takes different values at two points of , thenIn particular a continuous real function on  that is not\nconstant has positive seminorm on .\n\nMonotonicity holds because the integrand is nonnegative and the restricted\nproduct measure grows with the set. If , continuity gives a\nradius  such that both balls of radius  lie in  and\n for all  in the first ball and  in the second. The\nintegrand is positive on the product of the two balls, which has positive\nmeasure.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Fourier Sobolev Spaces\u0009Fourier and Fractional Sobolev Spaces","header":"4.5.1.8. The Gagliardo–Slobodeckij condition","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--The-Gagliardo___Slobodeckij-condition"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Certified-Bounds-and-Models/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Bounds-and-Models--One-Calculation___-End-to-End":{"contents":"The derivative request\n\n-- Retain the nonzero-domain condition\n-- for log in the report.\n#cas [derive x] (log x)\n\n\n#cas [derive x]\nDomain: x ∈ ℝ\nInput (scalar ℝ): (log x)\nOutput (scalar ℝ): 1/x\nConditions: x ≠ 0\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nillustrates the complete exact path. The parser first turns the ordinary\nnotation into an expression tree. Inspecting the logarithm records a nonzero\ncondition on its argument. The differentiator constructs the candidate ,\nand the analytic differentiation theorem justifies the ordinary statement\nthat the derivative of  at  is , provided .\n\nThe condition printed by the inspection command is part of the answer.\nTo use the derivative at a particular point, a theorem-facing tactic must\nprove that condition from the hypotheses. This is the same obligation that\nappeared in the affine Burgers denominator and in the logarithmic\nfundamental solution away from its pole. Polynomial examples need no such\nexclusion, but they pass through the same proof interface.\n\nA CEGA query follows the same architecture but ends in a quantitative\ncertificate. For\n\n-- Exercise the full parser-to-certificate\n-- pipeline.\nset_option linter.hashCommand false in\n#cas [enclose x in [-1 / 10, 1 / 10]] (cos x)\n\n\n#cas [enclose x in [-1 / 10, 1 / 10]]\nDomain: x ∈ [-1/10, 1/10] ⊆ ℝ\nInput (scalar map ℝ → ℝ): (cos x)\nOutput (certified interval in ℝ): [0.995004, 1]\nUpper bound: 1\nAbsolute bound: 1\nCenter and radius: ≈0.997502 ± 0.002498\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nthe parser again produces an expression tree, and a rational interval\nevaluator constructs candidate endpoints. A successful computation returns\nthose endpoints together with a proof that every value on the input interval\nlies between them.\n\nTaylor models use enclosures to bound their remainders while retaining\npolynomial structure. Derivative bounds also help determine a sufficient\nsubdivision for quadrature.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Certified Bounds and Models","header":"10.7.1. One Calculation, End to End","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Certified-Bounds-and-Models/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Bounds-and-Models--One-Calculation___-End-to-End"}});